Brinejack
Ingredients: NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Lead Guitar  
Creative Catalyst: The sound was discovered by deep-sea saturation divers repairing damaged transatlantic fiber-optic data cables along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.

Brinejack drags the kinetic, dance-floor-ready swing of late-80s street-soul four miles below sea level, fusing syncopated MPC groove shuffles with the terrifying, vast silence of the benthic abyss and the razor-sharp wail of electric lead guitar.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 98–104 BPM. The signature 16th-note swingbeat pulse is deliberately weighed down, exhibiting an eerie, hyperbaric drag—a heavy triplet lurch that mimics the sluggish fine-motor reflexes of saturation divers working under 400 atmospheres of pressure.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Aeolian minor modes spiked with unresolved major-7th soul chords and diminished suspense intervals. The mood vacillates between velvet R&B melodic warmth and freezing, abyssal dissonance.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Abyssal Repeater Latency Delay: Passing drum transients across live transatlantic submarine fiber strands, using the real-time ocean-floor electrical drop and acoustic conductivity to induce ghostly pitch micro-shifts.
  2. Sub-Atmospheric Gated Snares: Vintage 12-bit sampler snares routed through dynamic gates triggered by deep-water hydrophone pressure anomalies, causing punchy snare tails to abruptly suck backward into absolute vacuum.
  3. Galvanic Lead Reamping: Lead guitars captured with raw single-coils wrapped in copper foil and submerged in pressurized saline baths, producing a weeping, glassy sustain loaded with metallic hum.
- Dynamics: Claustrophobic and volatile. Tracks open with airless, isolated drum-and-bass tension like the inside of a sealed decompression bell before detonating into wide, cavernous choruses dominated by theatrical multi-track falsettos and soaring, phantom lead guitar solos.

Scene & Origin Lore
Brinejack crawled out of the pressurized living quarters of the C.S. Longitude, an offshore cable-repair ship stationed over the Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone in the North Atlantic. Saturation divers, confined to steel hyperbaric habitats for twenty-eight days at a time to bleed nitrogen from their blood, combated auditory hallucinations by tinkering with battery-powered grooveboxes and water-damaged electric guitars. When maintenance divers patched into high-bandwidth transatlantic data arteries to run diagnostic tone sweeps, the leak of high-energy commercial pop packets collided with benthic hydrophone feedback, creating an intoxicating, ghostly bounce. Back ashore in Aberdeen, Reykjavik, and Halifax, drysuit technicians and shipyard youth turned this into an underground dance culture. The fashion blends extreme subsea survival gear with late-80s swagger: neoprene boiler suits tailored into double-breasted blazers, high-visibility hazard straps worn like cummerbunds, bleached flat-top fades stiffened with seawater salt, and brass umbilical gas valves dangling as statement pendants. In coastal basements, dancers move in sharp, staggered swingbeat locks that collapse into sudden, weightless drifts.

Typical Instruments: Roger Linn MPC60 / Akai S900 samplers, Roland D-50 synth stabs, Fender Jazzmaster with submerged pickups, EBow lead guitar, drysuit solenoid clicks, hydrophone telemetry feeds, four-part layered falsetto vocals.  
For Fans Of: Guy, Cocteau Twins, The Durutti Column, King Krule  

